<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- No App Sandbox. Sparkle replaces the app in place, which a sandboxed
         app can only do through Sparkle's two XPC services; embedding and
         signing those in a hand-assembled bundle is more moving parts, and
         more ways for an update to fail, than the containment buys here.
         The app still runs under the hardened runtime, and the token still
         uses the user's login Keychain.
         Outside the sandbox the network entitlement means nothing, so it is
         gone with it.

         Library validation has to be off, though: under the hardened runtime it
         requires every loaded library to carry the process's Team ID, and a
         self-signed certificate has none. With it on, dyld refuses the embedded
         Sparkle.framework ("different Team IDs") and the app does not launch. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
